BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Short works by Cathy Lee Crane and Daïchi Saïto
DTSTART:20261116T230000Z
DTEND:20261117T045959Z
DTSTAMP:20261113T170946Z
UID:ec0960886cc93b96fa659d761999c4d3f1ad80bf@corning-events
SEQUENCE:0
CATEGORIES:Film,History & Heritage
DESCRIPTION:Event by Roy H. Park School of Communications\, Cinemapolis an
 d Mickie Quinn\nCinemapolis\nPublic\nThe Roy H. Park School of Communicati
 ons presents five short works and discussion from prestigious award-winnin
 g Film faculty Cathy Lee Crane and Daïchi Saïto in its next installment 
 of the Park Faculty Screening Series. Moving between haunted city streets
 \, contested borderlands\, and elemental landscapes\, this collection of f
 ilms examines the traces people leave behind. Blurring the boundaries betw
 een memory and history\, fact and fiction\, the works invite audiences int
 o immersive meditations on migration\, identity\, time\, and the power of 
 place.\nShort works by Cathy Lee Crane & Daïchi Saïto:\n⁃ The Girl fro
 m Marseilles\n⁃ Untitled (For Anthology)\n⁃ On the Line\n⁃ eartheart
 hearth\n⁃ terrestrial sea\nCathy Lee CRANE has crafted award-winning lyr
 ical films of speculative history for over 30 years with Fellowships from 
 the Guggenheim Foundation\, the New York Foundation of the Arts\, and the 
 New York Council for the Arts. She was named 2022 Artist-in-Residence at t
 he Farocki Institut in Berlin and her first survey was presented in 2015 a
 s part of the American Original Now series at the National Gallery of Art.
  Since 2017\, her multi-platform work Drawing-the-Line has produced two sh
 ort films\, a feature documentary\, and installations exploring the US/Mex
 ico borderlands. This work has been supported by the Rockefeller Foundatio
 n's Bellagio Center Residency Program & the El Paso Community Foundation. 
 She is Professor of Film at Ithaca College in New York.\nA 2025 Guggenheim
  Fellow in Film-Video\, Daïchi Saïto is an award-winning Japanese-born f
 ilm artist. Saïto divides his time between Ithaca\, New York\, and Montr
 éal\, where he co-founded the artist film collective Double Negative in 2
 004. His work explores the relationship between the corporeal phenomena of
  vision and the materiality of film\, fusing formal investigations of fram
 e and juxtaposition with sensual\, poetic expressions.\nThe screening will
  include a brief introduction and a talkback after the screening.\nThe Roy
  H. Park School of Communications has a distinguished tradition of multime
 dia storytelling. From narrative and documentary filmmaking to animation a
 nd experimental media\, Park faculty are creating impactful work that adva
 nces the field and enriches the classroom experience. This screening serie
 s showcases selected professional films and media projects by Park faculty
 \, celebrating the creativity\, innovation\, and artistic excellence that 
 define the Park School community.\n\nSource: FLXcalendar - https://calenda
 r.time.ly/o9b0uzpj/event/78515411/20261116180000
STATUS:CONFIRMED
URL:https://calendar.time.ly/o9b0uzpj/event/78515411/20261116180000
END:VEVENT
